articles.description:
A pricing page that exists in six languages usually ends up with six slightly different price tables. Someone updates the English plan limits, forgets the Japanese page, and now an AI assistant or a customer quotes the wrong number. The fix is to treat prices as data and render every language version from one source.
{
  "currency_by_locale": {"en": "USD", "de": "EUR", "ja": "JPY", "pt-BR": "BRL"},
  "plans": [
    {"id": "starter", "price": {"USD": 29, "EUR": 27, "JPY": 4300, "BRL": 149},
     "seats": 3, "features": ["chat", "reports"]},
    {"id": "growth", "price": {"USD": 99, "EUR": 92, "JPY": 14800, "BRL": 499},
     "seats": 10, "features": ["chat", "reports", "api"]}
  ],
  "updated": "2026-10-31"
}

